.TH "OSTRYA\-SERVE" "1" "" "ostrya" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ostrya\-serve \- Serve the repository over HTTP as an archive repository for pull
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ostrya serve [OPTIONS]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Serve the repository over HTTP as an archive repository for pull.
.PP
The repository can be in any mode that ostrya reads. In a mode other than \fB\%archive\fR, the server builds each \fB\%\&.filez\fR object on request. The server also answers the ref listing at \fB\%/_ostrya/refs/v1\fR, and has the receive endpoint of a push. The client groups of the repository config give the read rights and the push rights. The server identifies the client of each request by the auth items of the client groups: a bearer token, a Basic credential, a client certificate, the client address, or \fB\%anonymous\fR\&. A request that identifies no item gets \%401 or \%403 and no file. A push session takes the policy of the client group that carries the item in the namespace of its \fB\%Hello\fR: the local refs, or one collection. With no such group, or with a group that opens no push session, the session request gets \%403.
.PP
A ref under \fB\%refs/heads/\fR or \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR is served when the client group of the item has \fB\%r\fR on its name. An alias needs \fB\%r\fR on its name and on its target. The ref listing holds the local refs that the client reads, and with a \fB\%collection\fR pair in its query the collection refs that the client reads. A directory under \fB\%refs/mirrors/\fR whose name is not a collection id is not served and not listed. \fB\%refs/remotes/\fR, each other path under \fB\%refs/\fR, and \fB\%extensions/ostrya/\fR get \%404. Objects, deltas, \fB\%config\fR, and the other files under \fB\%extensions/\fR are served to each identified client. A denied path gets the \%404 of an absent path. With no client group, each client reads each local ref and each mirror ref.
.PP
\fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR give the summary of the auth item of the client, which \fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR writes. An item with no summary gets \%404. When the summary of the item does not match the client groups, the request gets \%404, and the command writes one \fB\%error:\fR line that names the item and tells the operator to run \fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR, one time for each item in each run. The responses of \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR carry \fB\%Cache\-Control: private\fR\&. Also with no client group, the summary needs the \fB\%\&.access\fR file of the \fB\%anonymous\fR item. A mirror pull removes that file, and \fB\%summary\fR gets \%404 until \fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR\&. \fB\%ostree summary \-u\fR leaves the file in place. In a repository where \fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR never ran, a \fB\%summary\fR that it wrote gets \%404. Else the server serves that \fB\%summary\fR while the file matches.
.PP
The server takes its settings from \fB\%[ex\-ostrya server]\fR of the repository config. Each option of the command with the name of a key has precedence over the key. A relative path in a key is relative to the repository directory, and a relative path in an option is relative to the current directory. \fB\%sign\-summary\fR has no option. At start, the command reads and checks the client groups, the server group, the trust groups, and the key groups. A bad group, or a key group whose key the command cannot read, ends the command with status \%1.
.PP
When all listeners are bound, the command writes the URL of each listener to standard output, one line each, and writes nothing else to standard output. Then it serves until the process ends. Warnings and errors go to standard error. For each push session that commits, the command writes one \fB\%warning: STEP: MESSAGE\fR line to standard error for each warning of the session. When a ref listing finds a directory under \fB\%refs/mirrors/\fR whose name is not a collection id, the command writes one \fB\%warning:\fR line that names it, one time in each run. With \fB\%[core] auto\-update\-summary=true\fR, each push that changes a ref writes each summary again, signed with the key groups of \fB\%sign\-summary\fR\&.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-listen\fR \fIADDR:PORT\fR
Listen on \%ADDR:PORT, an IP address and a port.
.IP
Give the option once for each address. The options replace the whole \fB\%listen\fR key. Without the option and the key, the server listens on \fB\%127.0.0.1:8080\fR\&. Port \%0 lets the kernel choose a port. The command refuses a host name. When all listeners are bound, the command writes the URL of each listener to standard output, one line each.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR
Serve HTTPS with the certificate chain in FILE, in PEM.
.IP
The certificate needs a key, from \%\-\-tls\-key or from the \fB\%tls\-key\fR key. ALPN selects \%HTTP/2 or \%HTTP/1.1. The command reads FILE once, at start, and refuses a file larger than \%1 MiB.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
The private key of the server certificate, in PEM.
.IP
The key needs a certificate, from \%\-\-tls\-cert or from the \fB\%tls\-cert\fR key. The command refuses an encrypted key, because it has no option for a passphrase. It reads FILE once, at start, and refuses a file larger than \%1 MiB.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-client\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Verify client certificates against the CA certificates in FILE, in PEM.
.IP
The CA needs a server certificate, from \%\-\-tls\-cert or from the \fB\%tls\-cert\fR key, or a header of a forwarded certificate, from \%\-\-trusted\-proxy\-client\-cert or from the \fB\%trusted\-proxy\-client\-cert\fR key. The CA verifies the certificate of the TLS handshake and the certificate that a trusted proxy forwards. A client that presents a certificate in the handshake must present one that the CA signed. A client that presents no certificate completes the handshake, and the other auth items identify it. A client certificate that the CA signed can match a \fB\%tls:*\fR, \fB\%tls:NAME\fR, or \fB\%tls\-sha256:HEX\fR item of a client group. The command reads FILE once, at start, and refuses a file larger than \%1 MiB.
.TP
\fB\-\-trusted\-proxy\fR \fICIDR\fR
Read the forwarding headers of a peer in the network CIDR.
.IP
CIDR is \%ADDR/LEN or a bare ADDR. A network with host bits set is refused. Give the option once for each network. The options replace the whole \fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR key. The option needs a header of the client address, from \%\-\-trusted\-proxy\-header or from the \fB\%trusted\-proxy\-header\fR key. From a peer in such a network, the server reads the client address from that header, the scheme \fB\%https\fR, and a forwarded client certificate. It walks the hops of the header from the right, and skips each hop in a trusted network. The first other hop is the client address. A proxy in the list must set or remove these headers in each request. No option removes the key: to trust no peer, remove the key.
.TP
\fB\-\-trusted\-proxy\-header\fR \fIHEADER\fR
Read the client address of a request from a trusted proxy in HEADER.
.IP
HEADER is \fB\%forwarded\fR for the \fB\%Forwarded\fR header of RFC \%7239, \fB\%x\-forwarded\-for\fR, or \fB\%x\-real\-ip\fR\&. The server reads this one header. With \fB\%forwarded\fR, the \fB\%proto\fR of the client hop gives the scheme. With the other two, the rightmost value of \fB\%X\-Forwarded\-Proto\fR gives the scheme. The option needs a trusted network, from \%\-\-trusted\-proxy or from the \fB\%trusted\-proxy\fR key. No option removes the key.
.TP
\fB\-\-trusted\-proxy\-client\-cert\fR \fIHEADER\fR
Read the client certificate of a request from a trusted proxy in the request header HEADER.
.IP
HEADER is a header name: an HTTP token of RFC \%9110, which is one or more ASCII letters, digits, and token characters. The value is a \%URL\-escaped PEM certificate, as nginx \fB\%$ssl_client_escaped_cert\fR gives it, or a \%base64 DER certificate, as HAProxy \fB\%ssl_c_der,base64\fR and Traefik \fB\%passTLSClientCert\fR give it. The client CA verifies the certificate, also on a plain HTTP listener. A value with more than one certificate, a value that does not parse, and a certificate that the CA does not verify get \%403. The option needs a client CA and a trusted network, each from its option or its key. No option removes the key.
.TP
\fB\-\-proxy\-protocol\fR[=\fIBOOL\fR]
Read a PROXY protocol header, version \%1 or \%2, at the start of each connection.
.IP
\fB\%\-\-proxy\-protocol\fR reads the header, and \fB\%\-\-proxy\-protocol=false\fR does not. The header comes before the TLS handshake, and its source address is the client address. The server closes a connection with no valid header, or with no header within \%30 seconds, with no response. Each peer that can connect states its own client address in the header, so only the proxy must be able to reach the listener. Possible values: true, false.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR[=\fIBOOL\fR]
Take a bearer or Basic credential over plain HTTP.
.IP
\fB\%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR takes such a credential, and \fB\%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials=false\fR refuses it. Without the option and the key, the server answers \%403 to such a request, also when a client group has the \fB\%anonymous\fR item, unless a trusted proxy states the scheme \fB\%https\fR (see \%\-\-trusted\-proxy). Use it for a server on a loopback address, or behind a proxy that terminates TLS and is not a trusted proxy. A read of the files of a pull takes the same rule. Possible values: true, false.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Abort a push session that stays idle for SECONDS.
.IP
A push session is idle when it has no request in progress, or when its request body delivers no byte. The value is a positive integer. Without the option and the key, the value is \%300.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-sessions\fR \fIN\fR
Serve at most N push sessions at the same time.
.IP
The value is a positive integer. Without the option and the key, the value is \%16. A request that opens a session past this limit gets the HTTP status \%503.
.TP
\fB\-\-parallel\-uploads\fR \fIN\fR
Let a push session send N object streams at the same time.
.IP
The value is \%1 to \%31. Without the option and the key, the value is \%4. The \fB\%HelloReply\fR of a session announces it. Over \%HTTP/2, the receive window of a stream is \%2 MiB, and the window of a connection is N times \%2 MiB.
.TP
\fB\-\-body\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
End a connection whose response waits more than SECONDS for the client.
.IP
The response waits for the client to take its next bytes. If an \%HTTP/2 client does not answer a ping within SECONDS, it also loses its connection. The value is a positive integer. Without the option and the key, the value is \%60.
.PP
The global options are listed in \fBostrya\fR(1).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBostrya\fR(1)
